**`ci/pack_require_gate.cjs`** — pure, GraalJS-safe (var, no arrows, no template literals):

```js
'use strict';
/**
 * Pack-release require sanity gate (gh-812 ...).
 * Pure module ... loaded by ci/release_packs.mjs (node) and the dmtools test runner.
 */

var REQUIRE_RE = /\brequire\s*\(\s*(['"])((?:\.\.?\/)[^'"]*)\1\s*\)/g;

function requireSpecs(source) { ... }
function resolveRequire(fromPath, spec) { ... }
function unresolvedRequires(files) { ... }

module.exports = { requireSpecs, resolveRequire, unresolvedRequires };
```

Careful with the regex: `((?:\.\.?\/)[^'"]*)` — requires spec starts with `./` or `../`. `[^'"]*` then closing quote matched by backreference \1. Also must NOT match `require('./x.js')` inside strings/comments? Overkill — a static gate over-matching a bit is acceptable (worst case false positive → investigate). Keep it simple.

resolveRequire: normalize by splitting on '/', handling '.', '..'; join. Note the test: `resolveRequire('js/smAgent.js', './common//reviewVerdicts.js')` → 'js/common/reviewVerdicts.js'. Splitting 'common//reviewVerdicts.js' on '/' gives ['common','','reviewVerdicts.js'] — empty segment must be skipped. Handle: `if (seg === '' || seg === '.') continue; if (seg === '..') pop; else push`.

unresolvedRequires(files): build path set from files[].path; for each file, specs = requireSpecs(source); for each spec: target = resolveRequire(path, spec); if set has target → ok; else if set has target + '.js' → ok; else push {from, spec, target}.

Also what about specs ending with '/'? Unlikely; fine.

**`ci/pack_version_guard.cjs`**:

```js
function bump(version, kind) — same semver logic as release_packs.mjs
function resolveBaseVersion(ledgerVersion, shippedVersion) → shipped || ledger || '0.1.0'
function resolveCandidateVersion(base, bumpKind, affected) → affected ? bump(base, bumpKind) : base
function resolveShipVersion(candidateVersion, differs) → differs ? bump(candidateVersion, 'patch') : candidateVersion
function payloadFingerprint(manifestJson) → parse; files → sorted by path → 'path:sha256' joined '; parse failure → throw? If manifest unparseable, better to treat as null (can't compare)... Hmm: fingerprintsDiffer(null, x) = false per test. payloadFingerprint(null) should return null (guard). payloadFingerprint('garbage') → throw is fine (caller validates zips earlier anyway) — but let me be defensive: return null on parse failure, and fingerprintsDiffer treats null as "no comparison".
