Found a REAL bug in my code: `shippedCatalog()` returns `SHIPPED_CATALOG` which is initialized to `null`, and when there's no PREV_TAG (or DRY_RUN), it returns `null` — then `shippedCatalog()[agent]` throws. In the first E2E run, the download FAILED and set `SHIPPED_CATALOG = {}` → fine. In THIS run (no --prev-release-tag), `shippedCatalog()` returns null → crash.

Wait — how did the unit tests + first E2E miss it? First E2E: PREV_TAG set, download failed → SHIPPED_CATALOG = {} (the catch sets it) → `[agent]` on {} → undefined → fine. This run: PREV_TAG unset → early return SHIPPED_CATALOG → null → crash.

Why didn't the unit suite catch it? The wiring tests only grep strings, never execute main(). (The dmtools runner can't run the ESM builder.) Noted — the guard-test approach has this limit; that's what live rehearsal is for.

Fix: `shippedCatalog()` should return `{}` when unavailable:

```js
function shippedCatalog() {
  if (SHIPPED_CATALOG !== null || !PREV_TAG || DRY_RUN) return SHIPPED_CATALOG || {};  // hmm
```

Cleaner:
```js
let SHIPPED_CATALOG = null;
function shippedCatalog() {
  if (SHIPPED_CATALOG === null) {
    if (!PREV_TAG || DRY_RUN) return {};   // nothing to fetch — no shipped state
    ... download; on failure SHIPPED_CATALOG = {} ...
  }
  return SHIPPED_CATALOG;
}
```
With `if (SHIPPED_CATALOG === null) { if (!PREV_TAG || DRY_RUN) return {}; ...fetch... } return SHIPPED_CATALOG;`

And `shippedZipManifest` already guards `!SHIPPED_DIR → null`. Good.

